Description: Do not activate the windows restored at startup, and activate the window opened for the launch with the launch activation token on Wayland.
 GTK 3 reads the activation token Firefox is launched with from DESKTOP_STARTUP_ID and activates the first toplevel
 it maps with it. During startup session restore that is the first restored window shown, and the window opened for
 the launch, holding the URL or the start page, stays without the token. Firefox also asks the compositor to
 activate every other window it shows, and session restore focuses the windows it restores, to stack them as they
 were. While session restore runs at startup, no event of the user is behind these requests, so mutter activates
 none of these windows and marks every restored window as demanding attention instead. GNOME Shell then shows a
 notification for each of them, lists them first when switching windows, and Desktop Icons NG moved to the
 workspace of one of them at the next workspace switch.
 .
 The token is now removed from GTK at startup and given to nsGTKToolkit, whose token the first window shown uses,
 as it already does for a launch passed to a running Firefox. A toplevel created while session restore runs is
 marked until session restore finishes, and a window shown before then, which is a restored one, neither uses the
 token nor asks to be activated, also when session restore focuses it. A window opened for the launch is shown when
 session restore finishes (wayland-session-restore-show-waiting-windows.patch) and uses the token then. A token
 which no window used, because every window was restored, is dropped at that point, so that a window opened later
 transfers the focus to itself as usual.
